  <article class="prose">
    <h1>2027 401(k) Catch-Up Contributions (Age 50+)</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> If you turn 50 or older by December 31, 2027, you can contribute an extra <strong>$8,500</strong> above the $25,500 base limit, for a total of <strong>$34,000</strong> (or $37,250 total if you are ages 60–63).
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Calculate Your 2027 Catch-Up Paycheck →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>The December 31 Age Rule</h2>
    <p>
      You do not have to wait until your birthday to start making catch-up contributions. Eligibility is determined by the age you attain by <strong>December 31, 2027</strong>. If you turn 50 at any point during 2027, you are eligible for the full $8,500 catch-up starting on your first 2027 paycheck.
    </p>

    <h2>Why 64+ Returns to $8,500</h2>
    <p>
      Under IRC Section 414(v)(2)(B)(i), the enhanced $11,750 super catch-up applies only if you attain age 60, 61, 62, or 63 during the 2027 calendar year. Once you turn 64 by December 31, 2027, your catch-up limit reverts to the standard <strong>$8,500</strong> ($34,000 total).
    </p>

    <h2>Mandatory Roth Catch-Up for High Earners</h2>
    <p>
      If your 2026 FICA wages (W-2 Box 3) from the employer sponsoring your 401(k) plan exceeded <strong>$155,000</strong>, your 2027 catch-up contributions must be designated as Roth under SECURE 2.0 Section 603.
    </p>
  </article>

